            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Olynthiacs</strong><br>\nIn the Olynthiacs, another series of speeches by Demosthenes, he addressed the citizens of Athens to provide aid to Olynthus, which was under threat from Philip. These speeches emphasized the importance of supporting city-states that were resisting Macedonian forces. He urged for decisive and immediate action to prevent the fall of such cities, which would lead to the increase of Philip's power.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Demosthenes' Political Influence</strong><br>\nDemosthenes' influence in politics grew as he became a proponent of vigorous opposition to Macedonian expansion. Despite his attempts, Philip II's military and diplomatic skill often outmaneuvered the Athenian statesman. Demosthenes' career was thus marked by his continuous struggle to garner enough support to counteract the Macedonian advance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Crown Speech</strong><br>\nOne of Demosthenes' most famous speeches, \"On the Crown,\" defended his political life and policy decisions. He argued that his actions were just and in the best interest of Athens. This speech was delivered as part of a legal dispute with Aeschines, another prominent orator, who challenged the legality of a public honor bestowed upon Demosthenes.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
