          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Exile and Return</strong><br>\nDue to his persistent opposition, Demosthenes often faced hostility from factions within Athens, which led to periods of exile. However, his dedication to the city-state's autonomy never waned, and he used his time away from Athens to further the cause against Macedon. After Alexander's death, Demosthenes returned to Athens and continued his political endeavors.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Demosthenes' Tragic End</strong><br>\nDemosthenes' life ended tragically when he committed suicide in 322 B.C. to avoid being captured by Antipater's forces, after the Battle of Lamia. His death marked the end of an era for Athenian politics and rhetoric. It is often seen as a symbol of the defeat of Greek independence against the rise of Macedonian power.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Demosthenes</strong><br>\nDemosthenes' speeches have been studied for centuries as exemplary models of oratory. His methods in speech-writing and delivery are still taught today, showcasing the power of rhetoric in public discourse. His passionate commitment to Athenian democracy and his skills as an orator have left a lasting impact on history and the art of public speaking.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
